\q1
\v 16 He called for a famine in that land—
\q1 all food supply chains were broken.
\q1
\v 17 He sent a man on ahead of them,
\q1 \add when\add* Joseph was sold as a slave.
\q1
\v 18 His feet were shackled together.
\q1 His neck was placed in an iron \add collar\add*
\q1
\v 19 until Yahweh’s\f + \fr 105:19a \ft It’s also possible to interpret the pronoun here as referring to Joseph, as in some other translations.\f* order came.
\q1 Yahweh’s promise\f + \fr 105:19b \ft It’s not clear what saying of Yahweh is being referred to here, so other translations will likely differ.\f* tested Joseph.
\q1
\v 20 \add Then\add* the king sent servants to release him.
\q1 The people’s ruler set him free.
\q1
\v 21 He made him the master of his household,
\q1 and the ruler over all his possessions—
\q1
\v 22 to instruct his officials at his pleasure,
\q1 and to teach wisdom to his elders.
\q1
\v 23 Then Israel went to Egypt,
\q1 \add yes,\add* Jacob lived for a time in the land of Ham.
\q1
\v 24 Then Yahweh made his people have large families,
\q1 and made them much more powerful than their enemies.
\q1
\v 25 He caused them to hate his people—
\q1 to deal craftily with his servants.
\q1
\v 26 He sent his servant Moses,
\q1 \add and\add* Aaron who he’d chosen
\q1 →\add so they would deal\add* with it.
\q1
\v 27 They demonstrated signs from Yahweh \add while\add* with the Egyptians,
\q1 and miracles in the land of Ham.
\q1
\v 28 He sent darkness and \add the place\add* went dark.
\q1 They couldn’t argue against his commands.\f + \fr 105:28 \ft It’s not clear who is being talked about here, so other translations will likely differ.\f*
\q1
\v 29 He turned their water into blood,
\q1 and their fish died.
\q1
\v 30 Their land swarmed with frogs,
\q1 \add even\add* in the palace bedrooms.
\q1
\v 31 He spoke and a swarm of flies came.
\q1 Gnats \add spread throughout\add* the entire country.
\q1
\v 32 He replaced the rain with hail,
\q1 with fire flaming on their land.\f + \fr 105:32 \ft There was no fire mentioned in the Exodus plagues, hence some translations interpret this as lightning.\f*
\q1
\v 33 He destroyed their grapevines and fig trees—
\q1 shattering all the other trees.
\q1
\v 34 He spoke \add out\add* and the locusts came
\q1 and grasshoppers that simply couldn’t be counted,
\q1
\v 35 and devoured all of the vegetation in their region—
\q1 they ate up all the produce from the soil.
\q1
\v 36 He killed every eldest male in their land—
\q1 the star products of their strength. STUD PRODUCTS???
\q1
\v 37 He brought his people out carrying gold and silver,
\q1 No one among the tribes stumbled \add as they left\add*.
\q1
\v 38 Egypt was glad when they departed,
\q1 because they’d come to very much dread them.
\q1
\v 39 He spread out a cloud for a covering
\q1 and a fire to give light at night.
\q1
\v 40 They asked, and he sent quail,
\q1 and they filled up on bread from the heavens.
\q1
\v 41 He split a rock and water gushed out—
\q1 it flowed in the wilderness like a river,
\q1
\v 42 because he remembered his holy promise to his servant Abraham.
\q1
\v 43 His people were happy as he brought them out \add of Egypt\add*.
\q1 His chosen \add ones\add* were shouting with joy.
\q1
\v 44 He gave them the foreigners’ lands,
\q1 and they took possession of the peoples’ goods,
\q1
\v 45 so that they might follow his instructions
\q2 and obey his rules.
\q1 Praise Yah.
\c 106
\s1 Yahweh’s goodness
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yah. Give thanks to Yahweh because he’s good,
\q1 because his loyal commitment never ends.
\q1
\v 2 Who can recount \add everything that\add* Yahweh’s done?
\q1 Can anyone \add list\add* all his praises?
\q1
\v 3 Those who promote justice will be blessed,
\q1 as will those who always do what is right.
\q1
\v 4 When you help your people, Yahweh, remember me.
\q1 Help me when you rescue them.
\q2
\v 5 \add I want\add* to see your chosen \add people\add* prosper—
\q1 be happy that your nation is happy
\q1 \add and\add* share the splendour of your inheritance.
\q1
\v 6 We have disobeyed God like our ancestors did.
\q1 We have done things that were wrong, and have acted wickedly.
\q1
\v 7 Our ancestors didn’t appreciate the miracles you did in Egypt.
\q1 They \add just\add* forgot about your display of loyal commitment,
\q1 and \add instead\add* they rebelled \add down there\add* at the Red sea.
\q1
\v 8 However, he saved them for the sake of his reputation—
\q1 to make his incredible power known \add to the world\add*.
\q1
\v 9 He commanded the Red sea and it dried up,
\q1 then he led them through its depths as if it was ordinary land
\q1
\v 10 and saved them from the forces of those who hated them—
\q1 yes, he rescued them from their enemy’s power.
\q1
\v 11 The water came back over their enemies—
\q1 there wasn’t a \add single\add* one of them left.
\q1
\v 12 Then they \add finally\add* believed his words,
\q1 and they sang praises to him.
\b
\q1
\v 13 But they soon forgot what he’d done.
\q1 They didn’t \add bother\add* waiting for his advice.
\q1
\v 14 They had intense cravings \add for meat\add* in the wilderness,
\q1 and they challenged God \add right there\add* in the desert.
\q1
\v 15 So he gave them what they asked for
\q1 but he sent them a horrible disease \add with it\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 16 Then in the camp, they became jealous of Moses,
\q1 and Aaron, Yahweh’s chosen priest.
\q1
\v 17 The earth opened up and swallowed Datan,
\q1 and covered over Aviram’s followers.
\q1
\v 18 Then fire blazed among them,
\q1 and the flames burnt up the wicked people.
\b
\q1
\v 19 They made a \add gold\add* calf at Horeb,
\q1 and worshipped \add the metal\add* statue \add there\add*.
\q1
\v 20 They traded the splendour of their \add God\add*,
\q1 with a statue image of a bull that eats grass. \add 😀\add*
\q1
\v 21 They forgot about their saviour god
\q1 who’d done incredible miracles in Egypt,
\q1
\v 22 had done amazing feats in the land of Ham \add (i.e., Egypt)\add*,
\q1 \add and\add* had demonstrated powerful forces at the Red sea.
\q1
\v 23 So he said that he’d destroy them,
\q1 except that his chosen \add servant\add* Moses stood as intermediary,
\q1 to turn him away from his severe anger \add and plan\add* to destroy.
\b
\q1
\v 24 Then they despised that desirable region—
\q1 they didn’t believe his promise.
\q1
\v 25 Instead they grumbled in their tents
\q1 and didn’t listen to what Yahweh said.
\q1
\v 26 So he made his decision against them:
\q1 that they’d drop \add dead there\add* in the wilderness,
\q2
\v 27 and he’d disperse their descendants across many countries
\q1 and scatter them in \add distant\add* lands.
\b
\q1
\v 28 They submitted to the Baal of Pe’or,
\q1 and ate sacrifices \add that had been offered to\add* the dead.
\q1
\v 29 They provoked him to anger with their actions,
\q1 and a deadly disease broke out among them.
\q1
\v 30 Then Pinhas took a stand and intervened
\q1 and stopped that plague.
\q1
\v 31 That godly action was credited to him,
\q1 for all the generations to come.
\b
\q1
\v 32 They also made Yahweh angry at the Merivah springs,
\q1 and that led to trouble for Moses,
\q1
\v 33 because they’d rebelled against his spirit,
\q1 →and then he \add ended up\add* speaking rashly.
\b
\q1
\v 34 They didn’t destroy those people groups
\q1 as Yahweh had told them to do.
\q1
\v 35 Instead they mingled with \add those\add* other groups,
\q1 and imitated their \add ungodly\add* activities
\q1
\v 36 and worshipped their idols
\q1 which then became the reason for their destruction.
\q1
\v 37 They sacrificed their sons and daughters to the demons.
\q1
\v 38 \add Yes,\add* they shed innocent blood—
\q1 the blood of their sons and daughters
\q1 that they sacrificed to the idols and polluted the land with blood.
\q1
\v 39 Their actions made them unacceptable to worship God,
\q1 having prostituted themselves \add to other gods\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 40 So Yahweh got angry with his \add own\add* people,
\q1 and he despised his own inheritance.
\q1
\v 41 He allowed other countries to control them,
\q1 and those who hated them ruled over them.
\q1
\v 42 Their enemies oppressed them,
\q1 and subdued them under their control.
\q1
\v 43 He rescued them many times,
\q1 ^but they kept rebelling in their minds,
\q1 and were brought down by their \add own\add* sin.
\q1
\v 44 Nevertheless, he paid attention to their distress
\q1 when he heard their cry \add for help\add*.
\q1
\v 45 He thought about his agreement with them,
\q1 and he relented because of the strength of his loyal commitment \add to them\add*.
\q1
\v 46 He caused their conquerors to have pity on them.
\b
\q1
\v 47 Save us Yahweh our god,
\q1 and gather us \add back\add* from among other nations
\q1 →so that we can give thanks for your sacred reputation,
\q1 and praise you with real gladness.
\b
\q1
\v 48 May Israel’s god Yahweh be praised throughout the ages,
\q1 and all the people said, “Yes, let it be so.”
\b
\q1 Praise Yah.
\c 107
\ms1 Fifth collection
\mr (Songs 107–150)
\s1 Thanking for God’s goodness
\q1
\v 1 Give thanks to Yahweh, because he’s good,
\q1 and his loyal commitment endures forever.
\q1
\v 2 Let the people who’ve been redeemed by Yahweh speak out—
\q1 those he’s rescued from the enemy’s control.
\q1
\v 3 He has gathered them back from foreign lands,
\q1 from the east and from the west,
\q1 from the north and from the south.
\q1
\v 4 They wandered through the wilderness on a desert road
\q1 and found no city that they could settle in.
\q1
\v 5 They were hungry and thirsty—
\q1 fading away \add from exhaustion\add*.
\q1
\v 6 Then they called out to Yahweh in their \add time of\add* trouble,
\q1 →and he rescued them out of their distress.
\q1
\v 7 He led them by a direct path
\q1 →so that they could go to a city to live in.
\q1
\v 8 Oh that people would praise Yahweh for his loyal commitment
\q1 and for the amazing things he’s done for humanity,
\q1
\v 9 because he satisfies the longings of those who thirst,
\q1 and the desires of those who hunger, he fills up with good things.
\q1
\v 10 Some sat in darkness and in gloom—
\q1 prisoners chained up and experiencing suffering.
\q1
\v 11 That was because they had rebelled against God’s messages
\q1 and rejected the highest one’s instructions.
\q1
\v 12 He used hardships to humble them.
\q1 They stumbled and there wasn’t anyone to help them up.
\q1
\v 13 Then they called out to Yahweh in their \add time of\add* trouble,
\q1 and he rescued them out of their distress.
\q1
\v 14 He broke off their chains
\q1 and brought them out of darkness and gloom.
\q1
\v 15 Oh that people would praise Yahweh for his loyal commitment
\q1 and for the amazing things he’s done for humanity,
\q1
\v 16 because he’s broken the bronze gates
\q1 and cut through the iron bars.
\q1
\v 17 They were foolish in their rebellious ways
\q1 and made to suffer because of their sins.
\q1
\v 18 They lost their desire to eat any food,
\q1 and they came close to death’s gates.
\q1
\v 19 Then they called out to Yahweh in their \add time of\add* trouble,
\q1 and he rescued them out of their distress.
\q1
\v 20 He sent his message and healed them,
\q1 and he rescued them from their destruction.
\q2
\v 21 Oh that people would praise Yahweh for his loyal commitment
\q1 and for the amazing things he’s done for humanity.
\q1
\v 22 Let them offer thanksgiving sacrifices
\q1 and write songs about what he’s done.
\q1
\v 23 Some travel across the ocean in ships
\q1 and do business overseas.
\q1
\v 24 Those people saw Yahweh’s activities
\q1 and his miracles on the oceans,
\q1
\v 25 because he spoke \add out his command\add*
\q1 and aroused the windstorm that stirred up the waves.
\q1
\v 26 Their ships reached up to the sky,
\q1 \add then\add* they sank down into the troughs.
\q1 Their courage melted away in anguish.
\q1
\v 27 They swayed and staggered like drunkards
\q1 and had run out of sensible ideas.
\q1
\v 28 Then they called out to Yahweh in their \add time of\add* trouble,
\q1 and he rescued them out of their distress.
\q1
\v 29 He calmed the storm,
\q1 and the waves were stilled.
\q1
\v 30 Then they celebrated because it had quietened down,
\q1 →so he brought them to their desired harbour.
\q1
\v 31 Oh that people would praise Yahweh for his loyal commitment
\q1 and for the amazing things that he’s done for humanity.
\q1
\v 32 Let them speak about his goodness to the people’s assembly
\q1 and praise him at the council of the elders.
\q1
\v 33 He turns rivers into wilderness,
\q1 and springs of water into dry land,
\q1
\v 34 and a prosperous land into a barren place
\q1 because of the wickedness of those who live there.
\q1
\v 35 He turns the wilderness into a pool
\q1 and dry land into springs of water.
\q1
\v 36 He settles the hungry there,
\q1 and they build a city to live in.
\q1
\v 37 They sow fields and plant vineyards,
\q1 →so that they can bring in a profitable harvest.
\q1
\v 38 He blesses them so they are very numerous.
\q1 He doesn’t let their cattle decrease in number.
\q1
\v 39 They became less in number and status
\q1 through painful distress and suffering.
\q2
\v 40 He pours contempt on the leaders
\q1 and makes them wander in the wilderness where there’s no roads,
\q1
\v 41 but he protects the needy from affliction
\q1 and cares for his families like a \add shepherd with\add* his flock.
\q1
\v 42 The godly people will see that and celebrate,
\q1 ^and all wicked \add people\add* will be shamed into silence.
\q1
\v 43 Anyone who’s wise should take note of those things
\q1 and meditate about Yahweh’s acts of loyal commitment.
\c 108
\s1 Praise, then prayer for victory
\r (Psa. 57:7-11; 60:5-12)
\d A song by David.
\q1
\v 1 I’ve made my decision, God.
\q1 I will sing, yes, I will sing praises with sincerity.
\q1
\v 2 Wake up, lute and harp—
\q1 I’ll wake up the dawn.
\q1
\v 3 I will give thanks to you, Yahweh, among the people \add group\add*s.
\q1 I will sing praises to you among the nations
\q1
\v 4 because your loyal commitment extends to the heavens
\q1 and your trustworthiness reaches to the skies.
\q1
\v 5 Be honoured, God, above the heavens,
\q1 and may your splendour be displayed across all the world.
\q1
\v 6 Use your power to answer me
\q1 →so that those you love will be rescued.
\q1
\v 7 God has spoken from his sanctuary, “I will celebrate.
\q1 I will divide up Shechem and apportion out the Sukot valley.
\q1
\v 8 Gilead is mine, and Manasseh is mine.
\q1 Ephraim is my helmet \add and\add* Judah is my scepter.
\q1
\v 9 Moab is my washbasin.
\q1 I’ll throw my sandal over Edom.
\q1 I’ll shout in triumph over Philistia.
\q1
\v 10 Who will bring me into the fortified city?
\q1 Who will lead me to Edom?”
\q1
\v 11 It seems that you’ve rejected us, God?
\q1 You don’t go into battle with our armies.
\q1
\v 12 Give us help against our enemy,
\q1 because human help is worthless.
\q1
\v 13 We’ll win with God’s help.
\q1 He will trample down our enemies.
\c 109
\s1 My god help me
\d A song by David for the choir director.
\q1
\v 1 \add You’re the\add* God that I praise—
\q1 don’t be silent
\q1
\v 2 because the wicked and deceitful attack me.
\q1 They tell lies about me.
\q1
\v 3 They say hateful things all around me,
\q1 and they attack me for no reason.
\q1
\v 4 They pay back my love with accusations,
\q1 ^but I pray for them.
\q1
\v 5 They repay me evil for good,
\q1 and they hate my love.
\q1
\v 6 Appoint a wicked man to be over him,
\q1 then appoint an accuser to stand on his right.
\q1
\v 7 When he’s judged, may he be found guilty,
\q1 and may his prayer \add for mercy\add* be considered sinful.
\q1
\v 8 May his days be few—
\q1 may someone else take his office.
\q1
\v 9 May his children become fatherless,
\q1 and may his wife become a widow.
\q1
\v 10 May his children wander around and beg—
\q1 asking for handouts as they leave their ruined home.
\q1
\v 11 May the creditor take all he owns.
\q1 May strangers plunder what he earns.
\q1
\v 12 May no one extend any kindness to him.
\q1 May no one have pity on his fatherless children.
\q1
\v 13 May his children be eliminated—
\q1 may their names be blotted out in the next generation.
\q1
\v 14 May his ancestors’ sins be mentioned to Yahweh,
\q1 and may the sin of his mother not be forgotten.
\q1
\v 15 May their guilt always be on Yahweh’s mind.
\q1 May Yahweh cause their history to be totally forgotten.
\q1
\v 16 May Yahweh do that because this man never bothered to show any loyal commitment,
\q1 ^but instead harassed the oppressed, the needy, and the disheartened to death.
\q1
\v 17 He loved cursing—may it come back onto him.
\q1 He had no desire to bless \add others\add*—may no blessing come to him.
\q1
\v 18 He wrapped himself with cursing like a cloak,
\q1 and his curse entered his inner being like water—
\q1 like oil into his bones.
\q1
\v 19 May his curses cling to him like the clothes he wears to cover himself—
\q1 like the belt he always wears.
\q1
\v 20 May this be my accusers’ reward from Yahweh—
\q1 \add yes\add* for those who say evil things about me.
\q1
\v 21 Yahweh my Lord, deal kindly with me for your reputation’s sake.
\q1 Because your loyal commitment is good, save me,
\q1
\v 22 because I’m oppressed and needy,
\q1 and my heart is wounded within me.
\q1
\v 23 I’m fading away like a shadow in the late afternoon.
\q1 I’m shaken off like a locust.
\q1
\v 24 My knees are weak from fasting.
\q1 My body is frail from not eating enough.
\q1
\v 25 I’m scorned by my accusers.
\q1 When they see me, they shake their heads.
\q1
\v 26 Help me, Yahweh my god.
\q1 \add Exercise\add* your loyal commitment \add and\add* save me.
\q1
\v 27 May they know that you’re involved here—
\q1 that you, Yahweh, have done this.
\q1
\v 28 Though they curse me, please bless me.
\q1 When they attack, may they be put to shame,
\q1 ^but may your servant celebrate.
\q1
\v 29 May my opponents be clothed with shame.
\q1 May they wear their shame like a coat.
\q1
\v 30 With my mouth, I thank Yahweh frequently.
\q1 I’ll praise him \add even\add* in the middle of a crowd,
\q1
\v 31 because he’ll stand beside any needy person
\q1 to save them from those who condemn them.
\c 110
\s1 Yahweh and his chosen king
\d A song by David.
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh told my master, “Sit \add here\add* on my right
\q1 until I \add defeat\add* your enemies.”
\q1
\v 2 Yahweh will expand your power as king outwards from Zion—
\q1 you’ll rule in the middle of your enemies.
\q1
\v 3 When you \add exert\add* your strength \add in battle\add*,
\q1 your people voluntarily follow in full battle dress.
\q1 From the place where the dawn is birthed, your youth will be like dew to you.
\q1
\v 4 Yahweh has promised, and won’t change his mind:
\q1 “You’re a priest forever following the pattern of Melchizedek.”
\q1
\v 5 My master is helping right beside you.
\q1 He’ll crush kings once he gets angry.
\q1
\v 6 He will judge the nations \add and\add* fill \add them\add* with corpses.
\q1 He will crush the heads of many countries.
\q1
\v 7 He will drink from the stream along the road,
\q1 →therefore, he’ll be reenergised.
\c 111
\s1 Praise for what Yahweh’s done
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yah.
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1 I will give sincere thanks to Yahweh
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1 in the assembly of the godly people—in their gathering.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 2 The things that Yahweh does are amazing,
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1 eagerly awaited by all those who desire them.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 3 His work is majestic and incredible,
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1 and his righteousness endures forever.
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 4 He does wonderful things that will be remembered.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1 Yahweh is gracious and merciful.
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\q1
\v 5 He gives food to his faithful followers.
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1 He will always call to mind his agreement.
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 6 He showed his powerful works to his people
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1 in giving them the inheritance of \add other\add* nations.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 7 The works of Yahweh’s hands are trustworthy and right.
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\q1 All his instructions are reliable.
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 8 They are established forever,
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1 to be observed faithfully and properly.
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 9 He sent his people out of slavery.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1 He ordained his agreement forever.
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1 His name is holy and awe-inspiring.
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 10 Honouring Yahweh is the beginning of wisdom.
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1 Those who carry out his instructions have good understanding.
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1 His praise endures forever.
\c 112
\s1 The blessing for a godly person
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh.
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1 Any person who respects Yahweh’s \add instructions\add* will be blessed—
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1 anyone who greatly delights in his commands.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 2 Their descendants on earth will be powerful.
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1 The descendants of the godly person will be blessed.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 3 Wealth and riches are in their house.
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1 Their righteousness will endure forever.
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 4 Light shines in the darkness for the godly person.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1 That person is gracious, merciful, and just.
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\q1
\v 5 It goes well for the person who deals graciously and lends money,
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1 who conducts their affairs with honesty,
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 6 because they’ll never be shaken.
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1 The godly person will be remembered forever.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 7 That person’s not afraid of bad news—
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\q1 confidently trusting in Yahweh.
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 8 Their heart is tranquil, without fear,
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1 ending up looking out in triumph over their adversaries.
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 9 That person gives generously to the poor.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1 Their righteousness endures forever.
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1 They will be commended with honour.
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 10 The wicked person will see that and be angry.
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1 That person will grind his teeth in rage and will melt away.
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1 The desire of the wicked people will perish.
\c 113
\s1 Praising Yahweh’s goodness
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh.
\q1 Praise him, you servants of Yahweh.
\q1 Praise Yahweh’s name.
\q1
\v 2 May Yahweh’s name be blessed
\q1 both now and forever into the future.
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh’s name should be praised
\q1 from when the sun rises until it sets \add again\add*.
\q1
\v 4 Yahweh is high above all nations,
\q1 and his glory reaches above the skies.
\q1
\v 5 Who is like our god Yahweh who has his throne on high—
\q1
\v 6 who looks down at the sky and at the earth?
\q1
\v 7 He raises the poor up out of the dirt
\q1 and lifts the needy up from the ash heap,
\q1
\v 8 so that he may become one of the leaders—
\q1 one of the leaders of his people.
\q1
\v 9 He gives a home to the barren woman of the house—
\q1 he makes her into a happy mother with children.
\q1 Praise Yahweh.
\c 114
\s1 Praising God’s past miracles
\q1
\v 1 When Israel left Egypt
\q1 (Jacob’s descendants leaving a people who spoke a foreign language),
\q1
\v 2 Judah became his sacred place,
\q1 \add and\add* Israel his kingdom.
\q1
\v 3 The \add Red\add* sea looked and fled.
\q1 The Jordan \add river\add* turned back.
\q1
\v 4 The mountains skipped like rams,
\q1 \add and\add* the little hills like lambs.
\q1
\v 5 Why did you flee, sea?
\q1 Jordan, why did you turn back?
\q1
\v 6 Mountains, why did you skip like rams?
\q1 You little hills, why did you skip like lambs?
\q1
\v 7 Tremble, earth, before the master—
\q1 at the presence of Jacob’s god.
\q1
\v 8 He turned the rock into a pool of water,
\q1 \add and\add* the hard rock into a spring of water.
\c 115
\s1 Trust in Yahweh
\q1
\v 1 Not to us, Yahweh, not to us,
\q1 ^but to your name bring honour,
\q1 for your loyal commitment and for your trustworthiness.
\q1
\v 2 Why should the \add other\add* nations \add have to\add* ask,
\q1 “Where is their god?”
\q1
\v 3 Our god is in heaven—
\q1 he does whatever he wants.
\q1
\v 4 The \add other\add* nations’ \add gods\add* are gold and silver idols
\q1 that people have made with their own hands.
\q1
\v 5 Those idols have mouths, but they can’t speak.
\q1 They have eyes, but they can’t see.
\q1
\v 6 They have ears, but they can’t hear.
\q1 They have noses, but they can’t smell.
\q1
\v 7 They have hands, but can’t feel \add anything\add*.
\q1 They have feet, but can’t walk.
\q1 They can’t make any sounds in their throats.
\q1
\v 8 The people who make them are \add just\add* like them,
\q1 \add as is\add* everyone who trusts them \add to help them\add*.
\q1
\v 9 Israel, trust in Yahweh—
\q1 he’s your help and protection.
\q1
\v 10 \add You priests\add*, descendants of Aaron, trust in Yahweh—
\q1 he’s your help and protection.
\q1
\v 11 You who respect and strive to obey Yahweh, trust in him—
\q1 he’s your help and protection.
\q1
\v 12 Yahweh takes notice of us and will bless us.
\q1 He’ll bless the descendants of Israel.
\q1 He’ll bless the descendants of Aaron.
\q1
\v 13 He will bless those who honour him—
\q1 both young and old.
\q1
\v 14 May Yahweh give more and more children to you all,
\q1 and \add also\add* to your descendants.
\q1
\v 15 May you all be blessed by Yahweh
\q1 who made heaven and earth.
\q1
\v 16 The heavens, yes, the heavens belong to Yahweh,
\q1 but he’s given the earth to humanity.
\q1
\v 17 The dead don’t praise Yah,
\q1 nor do any of those who go down into silence.
\q1
\v 18 But we have decided to bless Yahweh
\q1 now and forevermore.
\q1 Praise Yah.
\c 116
\s1 Praise Yahweh who rescued me
\q1
\v 1 I love Yahweh because he hears my voice
\q1 and my requests for mercy.
\q1
\v 2 Because he listened to me,
\q1 as long as I live, I’ll call him \add for help\add*.
\q1
\v 3 The ropes of death surrounded me,
\q1 and the snares of the grave confronted me.
\q1 I was confronted by anguish and sorrow.
\q1
\v 4 Then I cried out to Yahweh:
\q1 “Please Yahweh, rescue my life.”
\q1
\v 5 Yahweh is merciful and fair—
\q1 our god is compassionate.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh protects those who are helpless.
\q1 I was in a desperate situation, and he saved me.
\q1
\v 7 My mind can relax again
\q1 because Yahweh has been generous to me,
\q1
\v 8 because you rescued my life from death,
\q1 my eyes from tears,
\q1 \add and\add* my feet from stumbling.
\q1
\v 9 I will walk in front of Yahweh
\q1 in the lands of the living.
\q1
\v 10 I believed in him,
\q1 even when I said, “I’m suffering badly.”
\q1
\v 11 In my distress I said, “All people are liars.”
\q1
\v 12 How can I repay Yahweh for all his kindnesses to me?
\q1
\v 13 I will raise the cup of salvation,
\q1 and call on Yahweh’s name.
\q1
\v 14 I will fulfill my vows to Yahweh
\q1 now in the presence of all his people.
\q1
\v 15 The death of his faithful ones
\q1 is costly in Yahweh’s view.
\q1
\v 16 Yahweh, I’m definitely your servant.
\q1 I’m your servant—the son of your servant woman.
\q1 You’ve released me from my chains.
\q2
\v 17 I will offer thanksgiving to you as a sacrifice,
\q1 and I’ll call out to Yahweh \add whenever I need help\add*.
\q1
\v 18 I will fulfill my vows to Yahweh
\q1 now in the presence of all his people,
\q2
\v 19 in the courtyards around Yahweh’s residence—
\q1 \add there\add* in the middle of Jerusalem.
\q1 Praise Yah.
\c 117
\s1 Praising Yahweh’s commitment to us
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh, all you nations.
\q1 Speak highly of him, all you people \add groups\add*
\q1
\v 2 because his loyal commitment towards us is extensive,
\q1 and Yahweh’s trustworthiness endures forever.
\q1 Praise Yah.
\c 118
\s1 Not ending the love of God
\q1
\v 1 Give thanks to Yahweh, because he’s good,
\q1 because his loyal commitment endures forever.
\q1
\v 2 Let Israel now say,
\q1 “His loyal commitment endures forever.”
\q1
\v 3 Let Aaron’s descendants now say,
\q1 “His loyal commitment endures forever.”
\q1
\v 4 Let Yahweh’s loyal followers say,
\q1 “His loyal commitment endures forever.”
\q1
\v 5 In my distress, I called out to Yahweh \add for help\add*.
\q1 →\add and\add* he answered me and set me free.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh is with me—I won’t be afraid.
\q1 What can anyone do to me?
\q1
\v 7 Yahweh’s on my side as my helper.
\q1 I will look on at those who hate me.
\q1
\v 8 It’s better to take shelter in Yahweh
\q1 ^than to put confidence in humanity.
\q1
\v 9 It’s better to make Yahweh your safe place
\q1 ^than to put your trust in your country’s leaders.
\q1
\v 10 All the nations surrounded me \add in battle\add*.
\q1 In Yahweh’s name I destroyed them.
\q1
\v 11 They surrounded me—yes, they surrounded me—
\q1 but in Yahweh’s name I destroyed them.
\q1
\v 12 They surrounded me like bees
\q1 \add then\add* disappeared as quickly as fire among brush—
\q1 with Yahweh’s help we defeated them.
\q1
\v 13 They attacked me to knock me down,
\q1 ^but Yahweh helped me.
\q1
\v 14 Yahweh is my strength and my song,
\q1 and he’s the one who rescues me.
\q1
\v 15 The cheerful shout of victory can be heard in the tents of the godly people—
\q1 Yahweh has demonstrated his power and strength.
\q1
\v 16 Yahweh’s power overcame—
\q1 Yahweh has demonstrated his power and strength.
\q1
\v 17 I’m going to live, not die,
\q1 and I’ll tell everyone about what Yahweh did.
\q1
\v 18 Yahweh has punished me harshly,
\q1 ^but he hasn’t given me over to death.
\q1
\v 19 Open the temple gates for me—
\q1 I’ll enter through them and then give thanks to Yahweh.
\q1
\v 20 This is Yahweh’s gate—
\q1 →the godly people enter through it.
\q1
\v 21 I will give thanks to you,
\q1 →because you answered me by rescuing me.
\q1
\v 22 The stone that the builders rejected
\q1 →has become the very important stone at the corner.
\q1
\v 23 That’s Yahweh’s doing—
\q1 →we think it’s just wonderful.
\q1
\v 24 Today’s the day on which Yahweh has acted.
\q1 →We’ll celebrate and be happy about it.
\q1
\v 25 Please, Yahweh, give us victory.
\q1 Please, Yahweh, send now success.
\q1
\v 26 Blessed is anyone who comes with Yahweh’s authority.
\q1 We bless you from Yahweh’s residence.
\q1
\v 27 Yahweh is God, and he’s given us light.
\q1 Tie the sacrifice to the corner posts of the altar with ropes.
\q1
\v 28 You’re my god, and I’ll give thanks to you.
\q1 You’re my god—I will praise you.
\q1
\v 29 Yes, give thanks to Yahweh because he’s good,
\q1 because his loyal commitment endures forever.
\c 119
\s1 Obeying Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1
\v 1 Blessed are those whose behaviour is blameless—
\q1 who follow Yahweh’s instructions.
\q1
\v 2 Blessed are they who keep the rules that he revealed—
\q1 who strive to sincerely obey him.
\q1
\v 3 Surely they wouldn’t promote injustice.
\q1 They do what he shows them.
\q1
\v 4 You’ve shown your principles through your instructions
\q1 →so that we should carefully follow them.
\q1
\v 5 If only I could be consistent
\q1 in keeping your rules,
\q1
\v 6 then I wouldn’t be ashamed
\q1 when I consider all your commands.
\q1
\v 7 I’ll give thanks to you because I want to do what’s right
\q1 when I learn about how you always judge fairly.
\q1
\v 8 I’ll keep your regulations.
\q1 Don’t totally desert me.
\s1 Internalising Yahweh’s principles
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1
\v 9 How can a young man keep his motives pure?
\q1 By obeying your message.
\q1
\v 10 With my whole heart I search for you with all my being.
\q1 Don’t let me stray from what you commanded.
\q1
\v 11 I’ve stored your message away in my memory
\q1 →so that I’ll be less likely to sin against you.
\q1
\v 12 You deserve praise, Yahweh.
\q1 Teach me your regulations.
\q1
\v 13 With my lips I’ve declared
\q1 all the judgements that you’ve pronounced.
\q1
\v 14 I get happier about everything you’ve revealed
\q1 ^rather than any kind of riches.
\q1
\v 15 I will meditate about your principles
\q1 and keep my eyes on your paths.
\q1
\v 16 I take delight in your regulations—
\q1 I won’t forget what you’ve said.
\s1 Pleased to obey Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 17 Deal kindly with your servant
\q1 →so that I can continue to live and obey what you’ve told us.
\q1
\v 18 Uncover my eyes so I can gaze
\q1 on the marvellous \add results\add* from your instructions.
\q1
\v 19 I’m a foreigner in the land—
\q1 don’t prevent me from hearing your commands.
\q1
\v 20 My inner being is continually crushed
\q1 with longing for your regulations.
\q1
\v 21 You rebuke the proud, who are cursed—
\q1 who stray from your commands.
\q1
\v 22 Spare me from disgrace and humiliation
\q1 because I’ve kept the requirements that you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 23 Even though rulers gather together against me and slander me,
\q1 your servant meditates about your regulations.
\q1
\v 24 Also, your revealed messages are my pleasure.
\q1 They are my counsellors.
\s1 Choosing Yahweh’s way
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1
\v 25 My inner being clings to the dust.
\q1 Speak out and give me life.
\q1
\v 26 I talked about my behaviour, and you answered me.
\q1 Teach me your regulations.
\q1
\v 27 Grant me insight into your principles,
\q1 →so I can meditate about your wonderful actions.
\q1
\v 28 My inner being melts away from grief.
\q1 Speak out and strengthen me.
\q1
\v 29 Keep me off the path of deceit.
\q1 Graciously give me your instructions.
\q1
\v 30 I’ve chosen the way of faithfulness.
\q1 I have desired your regulations.
\q1
\v 31 I cling to what you’ve revealed—
\q1 Yahweh, don’t let me be shamed.
\q1
\v 32 I run along the path of your commands,
\q1 because you have expanded my mind.
\s1 Prayer for guidance
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 33 Teach me, Yahweh, the way of your regulations,
\q1 and I’ll keep them to the end.
\q1
\v 34 Give me understanding, and I’ll keep your teaching.
\q1 I’ll observe it with all my heart.
\q1
\v 35 Guide me in the way of your commands,
\q1 because I delight in your way.
\q1
\v 36 Help me to want to obey what you’ve revealed
\q1 and not just want unscrupulous gain.
\q1
\v 37 Help me not to waste time looking at worthless things.
\q1 Encourage me to follow your ways.
\q1
\v 38 Fulfill your promises to your servant
\q1 →so that he will respect and obey you.
\q1
\v 39 Help me to be able to avoid the disgrace that I dread,
\q1 because your judgements are good.
\q1
\v 40 Listen, I long for your principles.
\q1 Encourage me to follow your ways that are right.
\s1 Trusting Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1
\v 41 May I experience your loyal commitment, Yahweh—
\q1 your salvation, according to your promise,
\q1
\v 42 then I’ll answer the one who mocks me with their words,
\q1 because I trust in what you’ve told us.
\q1
\v 43 Don’t totally prevent me from talking about your message of truth,
\q1 because my hope is based on your judgements.
\q1
\v 44 I’ll always obey what you taught,
\q1 forever and ever.
\q1
\v 45 I’ll walk freely,
\q1 because I strive to know your principles.
\q1
\v 46 I will tell kings what you’ve revealed,
\q1 and I won’t be ashamed.
\q1
\v 47 I take pleasure in \add obeying\add* your commands,
\q1 which I love.
\q1
\v 48 I will respect your commands, which I love.
\q1 I will meditate on your regulations.
\s1 Comforted by Yahweh’s principles
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 49 Remember your promise to your servant
\q1 because you have given me hope.
\q1
\v 50 That’s my comfort in my suffering.
\q1 \add Yes,\add* your promise keeps me alive.
\q1
\v 51 Arrogant people mock me terribly,
\q1 ^\add but\add* I haven’t turned away from your instructions.
\q1
\v 52 I remember your judgements from ancient times, Yahweh,
\q1 and I take comfort \add in that\add*.
\q1
\v 53 I’m seized by a hot anger
\q1 because of the wicked who reject your teaching.
\q1
\v 54 Your regulations are songs for me
\q1 in the house where I’m staying.
\q1
\v 55 I remember your name during the night, Yahweh,
\q1 and I follow your instruction.
\q1
\v 56 This is for me
\q1 because I accept your principles.
\s1 The loving of Law of God
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1
\v 57 Yahweh is who I’ve chosen.
\q1 I promise to obey what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 58 With my whole being, I ask for your favour.
\q1 Be merciful to me like you’ve promised you would be.
\q1
\v 59 I carefully evaluate which paths I’ll follow
\q1 and keep myself following what you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 60 I hurry and don’t put off
\q1 obeying your commands.
\q1
\v 61 The ropes of the wicked people entangle me.
\q1 I don’t forget your instructions.
\q1
\v 62 I get up in the middle of the night to bring praise to you
\q1 because your judgements are always right and fair.
\q1
\v 63 I’m a companion to all who strive to obey you
\q1 and apply your principles.
\q1
\v 64 The world is full of your loyal commitment, Yahweh.
\q1 Teach me your regulations.
\s1 The purpose of Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\q1
\v 65 You do good to your servant, Yahweh,
\q1 as you’ve said you would.
\q1
\v 66 Teach me good discernment and understanding,
\q1 because I trust in \add the wisdom of\add* your commands.
\q1
\v 67 Before my troubles came, I went astray,
\q1 but now I observe your promises.
\q1
\v 68 You are good, and you do good.
\q1 Teach me your regulations.
\q1
\v 69 The arrogant smear me with lies,
\q1 ^\add but\add* I myself keep your principles with sincerity.
\q1
\v 70 Their hearts are unfeeling like fat,
\q1 ^\add but\add* I myself delight in your instructions.
\q1
\v 71 It is good for me that I’ve suffered
\q1 →so that I’d learn your regulations.
\q1
\v 72 The instructions that come from your mouth are good for me—
\q1 more than thousands of gold and silver coins.
\s1 The justice of Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1
\v 73 Your hands created and formed me.
\q1 Give me understanding so that I can learn your commands.
\q1
\v 74 May those who respect you see me and be happy
\q1 because I put my hope in what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 75 Yahweh, I know that your judgements are fair and right,
\q1 and \add that it’s\add* in faithfulness that you make me suffer.
\q1
\v 76 Let your loyal commitment be my comfort,
\q1 as per your promise to your servant.
\q1
\v 77 May your grace come to me so that I may live,
\q1 because your instructions are my delight.
\q1
\v 78 Let the proud be put to shame,
\q1 because they’ve falsely accused me,
\q1 ^but as for me, I’ll meditate on your principles.
\q1
\v 79 May those who respect you turn to me,
\q1 and get to know what you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 80 May my conscience be blameless with respect to your regulations,
\q1 →so that I won’t be put to shame.
\s1 Requesting Yahweh’s intervention
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 81 My inner being faints with longing for your salvation.
\q1 I put my hope in what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 82 My eyes are exhausted \add with longing\add* for your promise,
\q1 saying, “When will you comfort me?”
\q1
\v 83 because I’ve become \add brittle\add* like a wineskin in the smoke.
\q1 I don’t forget your regulations.
\q1
\v 84 How long must your servant \add wait\add*—
\q1 when will you punish those who persecute me?
\q1
\v 85 The proud have dug pits \add to trap\add* me,
\q1 which is not according to your instructions.
\q1
\v 86 All your commands are sensible.
\q1 Those proud people pursue me with false accusations—help me.
\q1
\v 87 They have almost finished me off here,
\q1 but as for me, I don’t reject your principles.
\q1
\v 88 It’s your loyal commitment \add that\add* keeps me alive,
\q1 →so that I can obey what you’ve revealed.
\s1 Trusting in Yahweh’s principles
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1
\v 89 Yahweh, your message stands \add firm\add* in the heavens.
\q1
\v 90 Your faithfulness lasts throughout the generations.
\q1 You’ve established the earth and it endures.
\q1
\v 91 The heavens and the earth implement your regulations
\q1 because all \add things\add* are your servants.
\q1
\v 92 If your instructions hadn’t been my delight,
\q1 →then I would have died as a result of my suffering.
\q1
\v 93 I’ll never forget your principles
\q1 because through them, you’ve preserved my life.
\q1
\v 94 I’m yours—save me because I strive to follow your principles.
\q1
\v 95 The wicked wait for me in order to kill me,
\q1 ^\add but\add* I strive to understand what you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 96 I’ve seen a limit to all perfection,
\q1 ^\add but\add* your commands are very wide-ranging.
\s1 Appreciating Yahweh’s principles
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 97 I really love your instructions—
\q1 I spend all day meditating on them.
\q1
\v 98 Your commands make me wiser than my enemies,
\q1 because I’m always aware of them.
\q1
\v 99 I understand more than my teachers do
\q1 because I meditate on what you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 100 I understand more than the elders do
\q1 because I’ve followed your principles.
\q1
\v 101 I’ve avoided all evil behaviour
\q1 →so that I can obey what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 102 I haven’t deviated from your regulations,
\q1 because you yourself have taught me.
\q1
\v 103 Your promises are sweet to my taste,
\q1 yes, \add even\add* sweeter than honey on my tongue.
\q1
\v 104 I gain understanding through your principles
\q1 →so I hate all the different ways that people deceive others.
\s1 The peace/prosperity from law of God
\qa \bd נ\bd* (Nun)
\q1
\v 105 Your message is a lamp so I can see where I’m stepping
\q1 and a light to illuminate my path.
\q1
\v 106 I have promised and have confirmed
\q1 that I will honour your judgements that are fair and right.
\q1
\v 107 I’m suffering badly.
\q1 Keep me alive, Yahweh, as you’ve said you would.
\q1
\v 108 Yahweh, please accept the freewill offerings of my praise,
\q1 and teach me your regulations.
\q1
\v 109 My life is in continual danger,
\q1 yet I don’t forget your instructions.
\q1
\v 110 The wicked have set a trap for me,
\q1 but I haven’t strayed from your principles.
\q1
\v 111 I consider what you’ve revealed as my heritage forever,
\q1 because it gives me great happiness.
\q1
\v 112 I make your regulations my priority forever—to the end.
\s1 Safety in Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 113 I hate people who say one thing and do another,
\q1 but I love your instructions.
\q1
\v 114 You are my safe place and my protection—
\q1 I’ve set my hopes on what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 115 Get away from me, all you \add who choose\add* to do evil,
\q1 so that I can keep my god’s commands.
\q1
\v 116 Sustain me like you promised you would, so that I may live
\q1 and don’t make me ashamed of my expectation.
\q1
\v 117 Support me, and I’ll be saved,
\q1 →and then I’ll continually be paying attention to your regulations.
\q1
\v 118 You reject all those who stray from your statutes,
\q1 because their deceit is \add a form of\add* lying.
\q1
\v 119 You remove all the wicked from the earth like slag \add is skimmed off\add*—
\q1 →that’s why I love what you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 120 My body trembles in fear of you,
\q1 and I’m afraid of your judgements.
\s1 Obeying Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1
\v 121 I’ve done what’s fair and right.
\q1 Don’t abandon me to my oppressors.
\q1
\v 122 Guarantee your servant’s well-being—
\q1 don’t let the proud oppress me.
\q1
\v 123 My eyes are exhausted waiting for you to rescue me
\q1 and for your promise to free me of guilt.
\q1
\v 124 Deal with your servant according to your loyal commitment,
\q1 and teach me your regulations.
\q1
\v 125 I’m your servant—give me understanding
\q1 →so that I can understand what you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 126 It’s time for Yahweh to take action—
\q1 they disobey your instructions.
\q1
\v 127 That’s why I love your commands more than gold—
\q1 more than fine gold.
\q1
\v 128 That’s why I consider all your principles to be right in everything.
\q1 I hate every path that leads to lies.
\s1 Wanting to obey Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 129 What you’ve revealed is wonderful,
\q1 →therefore I obey it.
\q1
\v 130 The unfolding of your messages gives light—
\q1 it gives insight to the untrained.
\q1
\v 131 I open my mouth wide and pant
\q1 because I long for your commands.
\q1
\v 132 Turn to me and be gracious to me as usual—
\q1 to those who love your name.
\q1
\v 133 Guide me as you promised
\q1 and don’t let wickedness gain power over me.
\q1
\v 134 \add Pay the\add* ransom \add to free\add* me from oppression by others
\q1 →so that I may keep your principles.
\q1
\v 135 Look favourably on your servant,
\q1 and teach me your regulations.
\q1
\v 136 Tears stream down from my eyes
\q1 because others don’t follow your instructions.
\s1 Yahweh demands obedience
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1
\v 137 You always do what is right, Yahweh,
\q1 and your regulations are fair.
\q1
\v 138 What you’ve revealed demands obedience
\q1 and much faithfulness.
\q1
\v 139 My zeal has consumed me
\q1 because my enemies forget your messages.
\q1
\v 140 What you said is totally pure
\q1 and your servant loves it.
\q1
\v 141 I’m unimportant, and despised.
\q1 I don’t forget your principles.
\q1
\v 142 You do right and it’s right forever,
\q1 and your instructions are true.
\q1
\v 143 Distress and anguish have found me.
\q1 Your commands are my \add only\add* delight.
\q1
\v 144 What you’ve revealed is right forever.
\q1 Give me understanding so that I may live.
\s1 Calling to be rescued
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1
\v 145 I cried out with my whole being, “Answer me, Yahweh,
\q1 I will keep your regulations.”
\q1
\v 146 I call out to you—save me,
\q1 →so that I can keep what you’ve revealed \add to us\add*.
\q1
\v 147 I rise before the dawn and cry out for help.
\q1 I put my hope in your words.
\q1
\v 148 I stay awake through the night hours
\q1 in order to meditate on what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 149 Apply your loyal commitment to listening to my voice.
\q1 Yahweh, preserve my life according to your justice.
\q1
\v 150 Those pursuing wickedness are coming near.
\q1 They wander far from your instructions.
\q1
\v 151 You are near, Yahweh,
\q1 and all your commands are reliable.
\q1
\v 152 Long ago I learned from the things that you revealed
\q1 that you had established them forever.
\s1 Calling to be rescued
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 153 Take note of my suffering and rescue me
\q1 because I don’t forget your instructions.
\q1
\v 154 Plead my cause and buy me back.
\q1 Give me life as you promised.
\q1
\v 155 The wicked have no chance of getting rescued,
\q1 because they have no interest in your regulations.
\q1
\v 156 Your mercies are many, Yahweh.
\q1 Preserve me by means of your judgements.
\q1
\v 157 My pursuers and my adversaries are many
\q1 ^\add but\add* I haven’t turned from what you’ve revealed.
\q1
\v 158 I see the treacherous and despise them
\q1 because they don’t take notice of what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 159 See how I love your principles.
\q1 Yahweh, preserve me according to your loyal commitment.
\q1
\v 160 The essence of your message is truth,
\q1 and all your righteous judgements last forever.
\s1 Obeying Yahweh’s instructions
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1
\v 161 Officials pursue me without cause,
\q1 but my heart trembles with what you’ve said.
\q1
\v 162 I’m happy because of what you’ve promised,
\q1 like someone who discovers a large jackpot.
\q1
\v 163 I hate deceit, in fact I loathe it—
\q1 ^it’s your instructions that I love.
\q1
\v 164 I praise you seven times a day
\q1 on account of your righteous judgements.
\q1
\v 165 Those who love your instructions \add find\add* extensive peace—
\q1 nothing causes them to stumble.
\q1
\v 166 I put my hope in you, Yahweh, to rescue me,
\q1 and I do what you commanded.
\q1
\v 167 I sincerely follow all the things that you’ve revealed,
\q1 and I love them a lot.
\q1
\v 168 I keep your principles and everything you’ve revealed,
\q1 because you see everything I do.
\s1 A prayer for help
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1
\v 169 Let my cry \add for help\add* approach you, Yahweh.
\q1 Speak to me to give me understanding.
\q1
\v 170 May my request reach your ears.
\q1 Rescue me as you promised you would.
\q1
\v 171 May my lips pour out praise,
\q1 because you teach me your regulations.
\q1
\v 172 Let my tongue sing about your promise,
\q1 because all your commands are right and fair.
\q1
\v 173 Let your hand be \add ready\add* to help me,
\q1 because I’ve chosen \add to follow\add* your principles.
\q1
\v 174 I long for when you’ll rescue me, Yahweh,
\q1 and I enjoy your instructions.
\q1
\v 175 May I live and praise you,
\q1 and may your regulations help me.
\q1
\v 176 I’ve wandered off like a lost sheep.
\q1 Come and find your servant because I haven’t forgotten your commands.
\c 120
\s1 Asking Yahweh for peace
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 I called out to Yahweh when I was in distress,
\q1 and he answered me.
\q1
\v 2 Rescue my life, Yahweh, from lying lips—
\q1 from a tongue of deceit.
\q1
\v 3 You deceitful tongue \add of the enemy\add*,
\q1 what \add punishment\add* will Yahweh give to you,
\q1 and then what will he add on top of that?
\q1
\v 4 Sharpened arrows like a warrior uses,
\q1 along with burning coals of broom \add branches\add*.
\q1
\v 5 It was my loss that I stayed at Meshek,
\q1 I lived among the tents at Kedar.
\q1
\v 6 I’ve lived my life for a long time
\q1 with haters of peace.
\q1
\v 7 I’m \add for\add* peace,
\q1 but when I speak, they’re for war.
\c 121
\s1 Yahweh our protector
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 I look up towards the hills.
\q1 Where will my help come from?
\q1
\v 2 My help comes from Yahweh
\q2 who made heaven and earth.
\b
\q1
\v 3 May he not allow your feet to slip.
\q1 May your protector stay alert.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Listen, Israel’s protector doesn’t doze or sleep.
\q1
\v 5 Yahweh is your bodyguard.
\q1 Yahweh is beside you to shelter you.
\q1
\v 6 \add Now\add* the sun won’t harm you during the day,
\q2 or the moon at night-time.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Yahweh will protect you from all harm.
\q1 He will protect your life.
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh will protect your comings and your goings
\q2 \add both\add* now and then forever.
\c 122
\s1 Wanting peace for Jerusalem
\d A song by David while walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 I was happy when they said to me,
\q1 “Let’s go to Yahweh’s house.”
\q1
\v 2 Jerusalem, our feet are standing inside your gates—
\b
\q1
\v 3 Jerusalem, the city that was built with its protective walls
\q1
\v 4 which is where Yahweh’s tribes went up to
\q1 \add following\add* what was revealed to Israel to give thanks to Yahweh,
\q1
\v 5 because judgement thrones remained there—
\q1 thrones of David’s descendants.
\b
\q1
\v 6 Pray for Jerusalem to have peace and prosperity.
\q1 May those who love Jerusalem \add live\add* at ease.
\q1
\v 7 May there be peace within your walls—
\q1 peace within your fortresses.
\q1
\v 8 For the sake of my friends and relatives
\q1 I’ll say, “May there be peace within you.”
\q1
\v 9 For the sake of the house of our god Yahweh
\q1 I’ll strive for the good of Jerusalem.
\c 123
\s1 Praying to see mercy
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 I look up towards you—
\q1 you who rule from your throne in the heavens.
\q1
\v 2 Just as servants look to their master \add for his favour\add*,
\q1 and the maids look to their mistress,
\q1 so we look to our god Yahweh until he has mercy on us.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Have mercy on us, Yahweh, have mercy on us,
\q1 because we’ve been very humiliated \add by our enemies\add*—
\q1
\v 4 in fact we’ve had way more than enough scoffing from the arrogant
\q1 and contempt from the proud.
\c 124
\s1 Thanking for Yahweh’s protection
\d A song by David while walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Let Israel now say,
\q1 “If Yahweh hadn’t been on our side,
\b
\q1
\v 2 \add yes,\add* when we were attacked
\q1 if Yahweh hadn’t been on our side,
\q1
\v 3 they would have swallowed us up alive
\q1 when their anger raged against us.
\q1
\v 4 Then the water would have swept us away—
\q1 the torrent would have gone \add right\add* over us.
\q1
\v 5 Yes, then the raging waters would have gone over us.
\b
\q1
\v 6 We praise Yahweh
\q1 who didn’t allow \add our enemies\add* to tear us apart with their teeth.
\q1
\v 7 We’ve escaped like a bird out of a bird-catcher’s trap—
\q1 the trap’s been broken and we’ve escaped.
\q1
\v 8 We rely on Yahweh for our help—
\q1 \add the one who\add* made heaven and earth.
\c 125
\s1 Against wicked rulers
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Those who trust in Yahweh
\q1 are like Mt. Zion which can’t be shaken—enduring forever.
\q1
\v 2 Just like the mountains surround Jerusalem,
\q1 so too Yahweh surrounds his people, \add both\add* now and forever.
\q1
\v 3 Wicked rulers must not settle on the land allotted to the righteous,
\q1 otherwise those righteous \add people\add* might \add end up\add* doing something that they shouldn’t do.
\q1
\v 4 Do good, Yahweh, to those who are good
\q1 and to those who strive to do what is right.
\b
\q1
\v 5 But as for those who \add choose to\add* turn to crooked paths,
\q1 Yahweh will banish them along with those who do evil.
\q1 May peace be on Israel.
\c 126
\s1 The cheerful harvest
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 When Yahweh restored Zion’s fortunes,
\q1 we were like those who dream.
\q1
\v 2 Then our mouths were filled with laughter
\q1 and our tongues with singing.
\q1 Then they said among the nations,
\q1 “Yahweh has done great things for them.”
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh did great things for us.
\q1 How glad we were.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Restore our fortunes, Yahweh,
\q1 like the streams \add do\add* in the Negev \add desert\add*.
\q1
\v 5 Those who sow in tears will harvest with cheerful shouts.
\q1
\v 6 The man who goes out weeping, carrying seed to sow,
\q1 will return again with happy shouts—bringing his bundles of grain with him.
\c 127
\s1 Children are a blessing
\d A song by Solomon while walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 If it’s not Yahweh who builds the house, those who build it are just wasting their time.
\q1 If it’s not Yahweh who guards the city, the watchman stands guard uselessly.
\q1
\v 2 It’s pointless getting up early, then coming home late,
\q1 or working so hard to get your food,
\q1 because Yahweh provides for those he loves even while they’re asleep.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Yes, children are a heritage from Yahweh,
\q1 and the product of the womb is a reward.
\q1
\v 4 The children of a young man
\q1 are like arrows in a warrior’s hand.
\q1
\v 5 The man who has his quiver full of them is blessed.
\q1 He won’t be put to shame when he confronts his enemies at the \add community\add* gate.
\c 128
\s1 Prosperity for those who honour Yahweh
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Blessed is everyone who honours Yahweh—
\q1 who strives to follow what he showed us.
\b
\q1
\v 2 When you eat the product of your own hard work,
\q1 you will be blessed and prosper.
\q1
\v 3 Your wife will be like a fruitful vine in your house.
\q1 Your children will be like olive trees as they sit around your table.
\q1
\v 4 Yes, indeed, the man who honours Yahweh will be blessed.
\b
\q1
\v 5 May Yahweh bless you from Zion—
\q1 may you see Jerusalem’s prosperity throughout your whole life.
\q1
\v 6 May you live to see your children’s children.
\b
\q1 May peace be on Israel.
\c 129
\s1 Praying against haters of Zion
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Let Israel say:
\q1 “My enemies have attacked me often since my youth.
\q1
\v 2 \add Yes,\add* often since my youth they’ve attacked me,
\q1 ^yet they haven’t defeated me.
\q1
\v 3 Their ploughmen ploughed on my back—
\q1 they made their furrows long.
\q1
\v 4 Yahweh is righteous.
\q1 He’s cut the ropes of the wicked.”\f + \fr 129:4 \ft Probably meaning ‘the ropes used to enslave us’, but not certain.\f*
\b
\q1
\v 5 May all those who hate Zion
\q1 be humiliated and turned back.
\q1
\v 6 May they be like the grass \add that sprouts up\add* on a roof
\q1 then dries up \add and dies\add* before it can grow tall,
\q1
\v 7 so it’s no use to the harvester
\q1 or to the person tying up the bundles of grain.
\q1
\v 8 May no one who’s passing by say \add to those haters of Zion\add*,
\q1 “May Yahweh’s blessing be on you all,”
\q1 \add or,\add* “We bless you all in Yahweh’s name.”
\c 130
\s1 Yahweh can redeem us
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 I call out to you, Yahweh—
\q1 out from the depths.
\q1
\v 2 My master, listen to what I’m saying.
\q1 Pay attention to my pleas for mercy.
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh, if you kept a record of our disobedience,
\q1 master, who could stand \add before you\add*?
\q1
\v 4 But you do have a willingness to forgive
\q1 →so that you might be respected.
\b
\q1
\v 5 I wait for Yahweh \add to rescue me\add*—
\q1 I put my trust in what he’s said.
\q1
\v 6 I wait for my master \add to help me\add*—
\q1 more eagerly than the night guard looks forward to the morning.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Israel, put your hope in Yahweh.
\q1 Yahweh is merciful and very willing to get us out of our situation.
\q1
\v 8 He’s the one who’ll pay the price for all Israel’s sins
\q1 so we can be free.
\c 131
\s1 Explaining inner peace
\d A song by David while walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh, I’m not boastful or full of pride,
\q1 nor do I pretend to understand amazing things that are beyond me.
\q1
\v 2 Instead, I’ve worked to maintain inner peace and quietness,
\q1 like a child who’s been weaned, \add yet still trusts fully\add* in its mother.
\q1
\v 3 Israel, put your hope in Yahweh
\q1 from now on and forever.
\c 132
\s1 Yahweh’s resting place in Zion
\d A song \add to be sung\add* while walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh, for David’s sake, remember all the hardships he endured.
\q1
\v 2 Remember how he made a promise to \add you,\add* Yahweh—
\q1 how he made a vow to Jacob’s mighty warrior:
\q1
\v 3 “I won’t enter my home,
\q1 \add or\add* lie down in my bed.
\q1
\v 4 I won’t let my eyes close
\q1 \add or\add* let myself doze away
\q1
\v 5 until I’ve found a place for Yahweh—
\q1 a residence for the powerful \add one\add* that Jacob worshipped.
\b
\q1
\v 6 Listen, we heard that the sacred box was in Efratah.
\q1 We found it in Ya’ar’s fields.
\q1
\v 7 We’ll go into the place where he lives.
\q1 We’ll bow low in front of him where he sits.
\b
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh, come to the place where you can remain—
\q1 you and the \add sacred\add* chest that represents your power.
\q1
\v 9 May your priests wrap themselves in integrity.
\q1 May your faithful \add followers\add* shout happily.
\b
\q1
\v 10 For your servant David’s sake,
\q1 don’t just ignore the one that you’ve chosen.
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh, \add you\add* promised David and you won’t break that promise.
\q1 \add You said,\add* “I’ll put your descendants on your throne.
\q1
\v 12 If your sons keep my agreement and the instructions that I’ll teach them,
\q1 →then their children also will sit on your throne forevermore.”
\q1
\v 13 Yes, Yahweh has chosen Zion—
\q1 that’s where he wants to reside.
\q1
\v 14 \add He said,\add* “This is my resting place forever.
\q1 I’ll live here, because I like this place.
\q1
\v 15 I’ll bless Zion with generous provisions.
\q1 Her poor \add people\add* will have enough food to be satisfied.
\q1
\v 16 I’ll have her priests lead the people to be saved.
\q1 The faithful \add people\add* will shout aloud in their happiness.
\q1
\v 17 I’ll cause one of David’s descendants to become a great king there,
\q1 and shine the light on the one I’ve chosen.
\q1
\v 18 I’ll cause his enemies to be shamed,
\q1 and it’s his crown that will shine.
\c 133
\s1 Living peacefully together
\d A song by David while walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Listen, it’s \add really\add* good and pleasant
\q1 when \add the nation’s\add* people get on well together.
\q1
\v 2 It’s like having expensive oil on the head,
\q1 running down the beard—Aaron’s beard,
\q1 \add and\add* which \add then\add* runs down onto the collar of his robes.
\q1
\v 3 It’s like the dew on \add Mt.\add* Hermon
\q1 which falls on the mountains of Zion,
\q1 because Yahweh commanded the blessing there—eternal life.
\c 134
\s1 Lift your hands and bless Yahweh
\d A song for walking uphill \add to Jerusalem\add*.
\q1
\v 1 Listen, bless Yahweh, all you servants of Yahweh—
\q1 \add you\add* who stand in Yahweh’s house at night.
\q1
\v 2 Lift up your hands towards the sacred place and bless Yahweh.
\b
\q1
\v 3 May Yahweh, the creator of heaven and earth
\q1 bless you from Zion.
\c 135
\s1 A praise song
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh—praise his name.
\q1 Praise him, you servants of Yahweh,
\q1
\v 2 who stand in the house of Yahweh—
\q1 in the courtyards around our god’s house.
\q1
\v 3 Praise Yahweh, because he is good.
\q1 Sing praises to his name because it’s pleasant,
\q1
\v 4 because Yahweh has chosen Jacob for himself—
\q1 Israel as his very own.
\b
\q1
\v 5 You see, I myself know that Yahweh is great,
\q1 yes, our master is greater than all other gods.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh does whatever he wants to do
\q1 in heaven and on the earth,
\q1 in the seas, including all their deepest parts.
\q1
\v 7 He makes the clouds rise up from the end of the earth.
\q1 He makes lightning accompany the rain,
\q1 and brings the wind out of his storehouse.
\b
\q1
\v 8 He struck Egypt’s firstborn \add males\add*—
\q1 both people and animals.
\q1
\v 9 He did miracles and amazing things in your country, Egypt,
\q1 against Pharaoh and all his servants.
\q1
\v 10 He struck many nations
\q1 and killed powerful kings,
\q1
\v 11 Sihon king of the Amorites,
\q1 and Og king of Bashan,
\q1 and all the Canaanite kings.
\q1
\v 12 He gave their land as an inheritance—
\q1 an inheritance to his people Israel.
\b
\q1
\v 13 Your reputation lasts forever, Yahweh.
\q1 Your renown, Yahweh, endures throughout the generations
\q1
\v 14 because Yahweh judges his people,
\q1 and he’s compassionate towards his servants.
\b
\q1
\v 15 The nations’ idols are silver and gold—
\q1 the work of people’s hands.
\q1
\v 16 Those idols have mouths, but they can’t speak.
\q1 They have eyes, but they can’t see.
\q1
\v 17 They have ears, but they can’t hear,
\q1 and there’s no breath in their mouths.
\q1
\v 18 Those who make them will end up like them,
\q1 as will all those who put their trust in them.
\b
\q1
\v 19 Descendants of Israel, bless Yahweh.
\q1 Descendants of Aaron, bless Yahweh.
\q1
\v 20 Descendants of Levi, bless Yahweh.
\q1 All you who respect Yahweh, bless him.
\q1
\v 21 Blessed be Yahweh in Zion—
\q1 he who lives in Jerusalem.
\b
\q1 Praise Yahweh.
\c 136
\s1 A song of thankfulness
\q1
\v 1 Give thanks to Yahweh, because he’s good,
\q1 because his loyal affection \add continues\add* forever.
\q1
\v 2 Give thanks to the god of gods,
\q1 because his loyal affection \add continues\add* forever.
\q1
\v 3 Give thanks to the master of masters,
\q1 because his loyal affection \add continues\add* forever.
\q1
\v 4 Thank him—the only one who does great miracles—
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 5 Thank him who used his wisdom to make the universe,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 6 Thank him who spread the earth out over the waters,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 7 Thank him who made the great lights,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever:
\q1
\v 8 the sun to rule over the day,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever,
\q1
\v 9 the moon and stars to rule over the night,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\b
\q1
\v 10 Thank him who struck Egypt’s firstborn \add males\add*,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 11 He led Israel out from among the Egyptians,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 12 He led them with his powerful hand and outstretched arm,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 13 Thank him who divided the Red sea in two,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 14 Then he made Israel go through the middle of that sea,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 15 Then he threw Pharaoh and his army down into the Red sea,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\b
\q1
\v 16 Thank him who led his people through the wilderness,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 17 Thank him who struck down great kings,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 18 Thank him who killed powerful kings,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 19 Thank him who killed the Amorite King Sihon,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 20 Thank him who killed King Og from Bashan,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 21 Thank him who gave them the land as their inheritance,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 22 Thank him who gave an inheritance to his servant Israel,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 23 Thank him who remembered us when we felt so low,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 24 Then he rescued us from those who set themselves against us,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\q1
\v 25 Thank him who gives food to all living creatures,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\b
\q1
\v 26 Yes, give thanks to the god of the heavens,
\q1 because his loyal commitment continues forever.
\c 137
\s1 Mourning Jerusalem’s destruction
\q1
\v 1 We sat beside the rivers in Babylon—
\q1 sat and wept when we thought about Zion.
\q1
\v 2 We hung our harps there on the willow trees
\q1
\v 3 because our captors there had demanded that we sing songs,
\q1 and those who never stopped mocking us said,
\q1 “Sing us one of the songs from Zion.”
\b
\q1
\v 4 How could we sing a song about Yahweh
\q1 while we were \add captive\add* in a foreign country?
\q1
\v 5 If I forget about you, Jerusalem,
\q1 may my right hand forget \add how to do anything\add*.
\q1
\v 6 Let my tongue stick to my palate if I don’t keep remembering you—
\q1 if I don’t prioritise Jerusalem over my favourite pastime.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Remember, Yahweh, the day Jerusalem \add fell\add*,
\q1 how the Edomites said,
\q1 “Tear it down—tear it down to its foundations.”
\b
\q1
\v 8 You daughter of Babylon who will be destroyed—
\q1 blessed be the one who repays you for what you did to us.
\q1
\v 9 Blessed be the one who seizes your little ones
\q1 and smashes them against the rocks.
\c 138
\s1 A prayer of thankfulness
\d \add A song\add* by David.
\q1
\v 1 I will thank you, \add Yahweh\add*, with my whole being.
\q1 I’ll sing praises to you in front of the idols.
\q1
\v 2 I will bow down toward your sacred temple,
\q1 and I’ll thank you for your loyal commitment and for your trustworthiness,
\q1 because you’ve made your reputation and your message more important than anything else.
\q1
\v 3 When I called \add out for help\add*, you answered me.
\q1 →You gave me strength and made me bold.
\b
\q1
\v 4 All the kings of the earth will thank you, Yahweh,
\q1 →because they will hear what you’re saying.
\q1
\v 5 Yes, they’ll sing about Yahweh’s ways,
\q1 because Yahweh’s honour is outstanding,
\q1
\v 6 because although Yahweh is up high, yet he can still see the lowly,
\q1 and he knows the proud from far off.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Even when I’m walking with danger all around me,
\q2 you will preserve my life.
\q1 You actively oppose the anger of my enemies,
\q2 and you save me with your intervention.
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh will get revenge against them for me.
\q1 Your loyal affection \add continues\add* forever.
\q1 You don’t abandon the work that you started.
\c 139
\s1 God’s care and total knowledge
\d One of David’s songs for the choir director.
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh, you have examined me, and you know me.
\q1
\v 2 You know when I sit down and when I stand up.
\q1 You understand my thoughts from far away.
\q1
\v 3 You’ve watched where I go and where I rest,
\q1 and you’re familiar with everything I do.
\q1
\v 4 Before the words even get to my tongue,
\q1 Yahweh, you \add already\add* know everything that I’m going to say.
\q1
\v 5 In front of me and behind me, you surround me
\q1 to use your power \add to protect me\add*.
\q1
\v 6 That knowledge is too amazing for me.
\q1 It’s above me and beyond my reach.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Where can I go from your spirit?
\q1 Where could I run away to—away from your presence?
\q1
\v 8 If I ascend up to the heavens, you’re there.
\q1 If I make my bed in the grave, wow, you’re \add even\add* there.
\q1
\v 9 If I flew up high with the dawning sun
\q1 or stayed down in a remote part of the ocean,
\q1
\v 10 you’d still be there to lead me with your hand
\q1 and you would help me.
\q1
\v 11 If I said, “Surely the darkness will cover me,
\q1 and the light will become night around me,”
\q1
\v 12 even the darkness wouldn’t be dark to you,
\q1 and the night would shine like the day.
\q1 The darkness is like the light to you.
\b
\q1
\v 13 Yes, you created my internal parts—
\q1 you put me together inside my mother’s womb.
\q1
\v 14 I’ll praise you because it’s incredible how my body is made.
\q1 \add Everything\add* you do is wonderful.
\q1 My inner being knows that very well.
\q1
\v 15 My bones weren’t hidden from you when I was made in private—
\q1 when I was woven together in the depths of the earth.
\q1
\v 16 Your eyes saw my unformed body.
\q1 All my history was \add already\add* recorded in your book
\q1 even before any of those days had started.
\q1
\v 17 Your thoughts are so precious to me, God.
\q1 They add up to something incredible.
\q1
\v 18 If I counted them, there’d be more than the sand \add grains\add*.
\q1 When I wake up, I’m still with you.
\b
\q1
\v 19 If only you’d kill the wicked, God
\q1 (and you violent men, get away from me),
\q1
\v 20 because they speak against you due to their evil desires—
\q1 your enemies lie \add about you\add*.
\q1
\v 21 Yahweh, I certainly hate those who hate you.
\q1 I despise those who rebel against you.
\q1
\v 22 I hate them completely—
\q1 they’ve become my enemies.
\b
\q1
\v 23 Examine me, God, and know my inner feelings.
\q1 Test me and know my thoughts.
\q1
\v 24 See if there is any wicked way in me,
\q1 and lead me on the timeless path.
\c 140
\s1 A prayer for protection
\d A song by David for the choir director.
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh, rescue me from wicked men.
\q1 Keep me safe from men of violence,
\q1
\v 2 because they plan evil in their hearts.
\q1 They assemble for battles every day.
\q1
\v 3 They sharpen their tongues like snakes.
\q1 Vipers’ poison is on their lips. \qs (Instrumental break.)\qs*
\b
\q1
\v 4 Keep me from the hands of the wicked, Yahweh.
\q1 Protect me from violent men who plan to ruin me.
\q1
\v 5 The proud have set a trap for me,
\q1 and they’ve spread a net with ropes.
\q1 They’ve set traps for me along the path. \qs (Instrumental break.)\qs*
\b
\q1
\v 6 I said to Yahweh, “You’re my god.
\q1 Yahweh, listen to my cries for mercy.”
\q1
\v 7 Yahweh, my master, you’re the strong one who can save me.
\q1 You protect my head during the battle.
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh, don’t let the wicked get what they want.
\q1 Don’t allow their evil plans to succeed, or let them attack. \qs (Instrumental break.)\qs*
\b
\q1
\v 9 Those who surround me raise their heads.
\q1 Let the mischief from their own lips bounce back on them.
\q1
\v 10 Let burning coals fall on them.
\q1 Throw them into the fire \add or\add* into muddy pits.
\q1 →Never again will they try to attack.
\q1
\v 11 May no agitator become established in the land.
\q1 May evil speedily hunt down the man planning violence.
\b
\q1
\v 12 I know that Yahweh will act with favour for the poor
\q1 \add and with\add* justice to the needy.
\q1
\v 13 Surely the godly people will praise your name.
\q1 The upright will live \add peaceably\add* in your presence.
\c 141
\s1 Accepting correction
\d A song by David.
\q1
\v 1 Yahweh, I called out to you. Come quickly to me.
\q1 Listen to my voice when I call to you.
\q1
\v 2 May my prayer rise to your face like incense.
\q1 May my lifted hands be like the evening sacrifice.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh, place a guard over my mouth.
\q1 Guard the door which is my lips.
\q1
\v 4 Don’t let my heart bend toward any evil thing
\q1 or participate in sinful activities
\q1 with men who behave wickedly.
\q1 May I not eat any of their delicacies.
\b
\q1
\v 5 Let a righteous person hit me—it would be a kindness.
\q1 Let them correct me—it will be \add soothing\add* like lotion on my head.
\q1 Help me not to be defiant,
\q1 because my prayer is always against those who do wicked things.
\q1
\v 6 \add When\add* their rulers are thrown down from the cliffs,
\q1 the people will listen to my words \add because\add* they’re pleasant.
\q1
\v 7 Like when someone ploughs and breaks up the ground,
\q1 so our bones have been scattered at the entrance to the grave.
\b
\q1
\v 8 My eyes are on you, Yahweh, my master.
\q1 You’re my place of protection—don’t abandon my life.
\q1
\v 9 Keep me from the trap that they’ve laid for me—
\q1 from the nets of those who do evil.
\q1
\v 10 Let the wicked fall into their own nets
\q1 while I escape.
\c 142
\s1 A prayer for protection
\d A song\f + \fr 142:0 \ft In Hebrew, ‘maskil’ is perhaps the name of this class of song.\f* and prayer of David when he was \add hiding\add* in a cave.
\q1
\v 1 I cry out to Yahweh with my voice.
\q1 With my voice I plead to Yahweh for his favour.
\q1
\v 2 I pour out my sorrows to him.
\q1 I declare my troubles in front of him.
\q1
\v 3 When my spirit is weak within me, you yourself know my path.
\q1 They’ve hidden a trap for me along the path that I walk.
\q1
\v 4 I look around and see that no one takes any notice of me.
\q1 There’s no \add safe\add* place for me to escape to
\q1 \add and\add* there’s no one who cares for my life.
\b
\q1
\v 5 I called out to you \add for help\add*, Yahweh.
\q1 I said that you’re my protection—
\q1 my portion in the land of the living.
\q1
\v 6 Listen to my cries \add for help\add* because I’ve been driven into a bad situation.
\q1 Rescue me from my pursuers because they’re stronger than I am.
\q1
\v 7 Bring my life out of prison in order to praise your name.
\q1 The godly people will gather around me because you’ve been good to me.
\c 143
\s1 A prayer to be rescued
\d A song by David.
\q1
\v 1 Hear my prayer, Yahweh—listen to my requests.
\q1 On account of your faithfulness, answer me in your righteousness.
\q1
\v 2 Don’t judge \add me\add*, your servant,
\q1 because no living person is innocent \add anyway\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 3 The enemy has pursued my life.
\q1 He’s smashed me into the ground.
\q1 He’s \add forced\add* me to live in darkness like those who’ve been dead a long time.
\q1
\v 4 My spirit is getting weaker.
\q1 My mind is full of despair.
\b
\q1
\v 5 I remember the \add good\add* old days.
\q1 I meditate about your activities.
\q1 I consider \add all\add* the things you’ve done.
\q1
\v 6 I spread my hands out towards you.
\q1 I long for you like a thirsty person longs for water in the desert. \qs (Instrumental break.)\qs*
\b
\q1
\v 7 Answer me quickly, Yahweh, because my spirit is weak.
\q1 Don’t hide yourself from me or I’ll become like those going down into the pit.
\q1
\v 8 Every morning I want to hear about your loyal commitment
\q1 because I’ve put my trust in you.
\q1 Show me the way I should go
\q1 because I’ve committed my life to you.
\q1
\v 9 Rescue me from my enemies, Yahweh—
\q1 I trust you as my protector.
\q1
\v 10 Teach me to do what you like
\q1 because you’re my god.
\q1 May your good spirit lead me
\q1 \add out of the valley\add* onto level ground.
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh, keep me alive for the sake of your reputation.
\q1 Rescue me from trouble because you \add always\add* do what is right.
\q1
\v 12 Apply your loyal commitment \add and\add* cut off my enemies.
\q1 Destroy all those \add threatening\add* my life, because I’m your servant.
\c 144
\s1 The thanking due to victory
\d \add A song\add* by David.
\q1
\v 1 Blessed be Yahweh, my stable base,
\q1 who trains my hands for war
\q1 and my fingers for battle.
\q1
\v 2 You’re my fortress and show your loyal commitment to me.
\q1 You’re my high tower and the one who rescues me,
\q1 my shield and the one I trust for my protection—
\q1 the one who subdues \add other\add* nations under me.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh, what’s humankind that you take notice of them?
\q1 Why do you even consider humanity?
\q1
\v 4 People are like a puff of air—
\q1 here one minute and gone the next.
\b
\q1
\v 5 Yahweh, cause your sky to bend over and come down.
\q1 Touch the mountains and make them active.
\q1
\v 6 Flash your lightning and scatter your enemies.
\q1 Send your arrows and drive them away.
\q1
\v 7 Reach your hand down from above.
\q1 Save me and rescue me from the surging waters—
\q1 \add away\add* from being controlled by foreigners
\q1
\v 8 who always tell lies,
\q1 even when they promise to tell the truth.
\b
\q1
\v 9 I will sing a new song to you, God.
\q1 I’ll play the ten-stringed instrument for you
\q1
\v 10 who saves kings \add from their enemies\add*—
\q1 \add the one\add* who rescued his servant David from a deadly sword.
\q1
\v 11 Rescue me from being controlled by foreigners
\q1 who always tell lies,
\q1 even when they promise to tell the truth.
\q1
\v 12 May our sons be like plants that grow large even when they’re still young
\q1 and our daughters grow like carved corner pillars in a palace.
\q1
\v 13 May our storehouses be full with every kind of produce,
\q1 and may the sheep in our fields produce thousands, even tens of thousands.
\q1
\v 14 May our oxen have many young.
\q1 No one will break through, no one will go out,
\q1 and no one will cry in our streets.
\q1
\v 15 The people that that happens to are blessed.
\q1 Blessed are the people for whom Yahweh is their God.
\c 145
\s1 A praise song
\d A praise \add song\add* by David.
\qa \bd א\bd* (Alef)
\q1
\v 1 I will highly commend you, my god, the King.
\q1 I will praise your name without ending.
\qa \bd ב\bd* (Beyt)
\q1
\v 2 Every day I’ll \add make sure to\add* bless you.
\q1 I will praise your name forever and ever.
\qa \bd ג\bd* (Gimel)
\q1
\v 3 Yahweh is great and deserving of a lot of praise—
\q1 We can’t fully comprehend his greatness.
\qa \bd ד\bd* (Dalet)
\q1
\v 4 Generation after generation will celebrate because of what you’ve done
\q1 and they’ll tell others about your powerful actions.
\qa \bd ה\bd* (He)
\q1
\v 5 I’ll meditate on your incredible splendour and honour
\q1 as well as \add all\add* the marvellous things that you’ve done.
\qa \bd ו\bd* (Vav)
\q1
\v 6 They’ll tell about how your awesome actions show your power,
\q1 and I’ll speak about your greatness.
\qa \bd ז\bd* (Zayin)
\q1
\v 7 They’ll remember the fame of your extravagant goodness,
\q1 and they’ll happily sing about your righteousness.
\qa \bd ח\bd* (Het)
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh is gracious and merciful,
\q1 slow to anger and frequently displaying his loyal commitment.
\qa \bd ט\bd* (Tet)
\q1
\v 9 Yahweh is good to everyone,
\q1 and desires the best for everything he made.
\b
\qa \bd י\bd* (Yod)
\q1
\v 10 Everyone you’ve made will give thanks to you, Yahweh.
\q1 Your faithful \add supporters\add* will bless you.
\qa \bd כ\bd* (Kaf)
\q1
\v 11 They’ll tell about the splendour of your kingdom,
\q1 and they’ll speak about your power,
\qa \bd ל\bd* (Lamed)
\q1
\v 12 making his powerful actions known to humanity’s children,
\q1 along with the incredible splendour of his kingdom.
\qa \bd מ\bd* (Mem)
\q1
\v 13 Your kingdom will continue forever,
\q1 and you’ll rule generation after generation.
\qa \bd (נ)\bd* (Nun)
\qa \bd ס\bd* (Samek)
\q1
\v 14 Yahweh supports all those who are falling
\q1 and raises up all those who are bent over \add under heavy loads\add*.
\qa \bd ע\bd* (Ayin)
\q1
\v 15 Everyone and everything’s eyes are watching out for you.
\q1 You give them their food at the proper time.
\qa \bd פ\bd* (Pe)
\q1
\v 16 You open your hand
\q1 and satisfy the desire of every living thing.
\qa \bd צ\bd* (Tsade)
\q1
\v 17 Yahweh is righteous in all his ways
\q1 and shows kindness in everything he does.
\qa \bd ק\bd* (Qof)
\q1
\v 18 Yahweh is near to all who call to him—
\q1 to all who sincerely request his help.
\qa \bd ר\bd* (Resh)
\q1
\v 19 For those who strive to obey him, he does what they desire,
\q1 and he hears their call for help and saves them.
\qa \bd ש\bd* (Sin/Shin)
\q1
\v 20 Yahweh protects all those who love him,
\q1 but he’ll destroy all the wicked.
\b
\qa \bd ת\bd* (Tav)
\q1
\v 21 My mouth speaks the praise of Yahweh.
\q1 Let everybody bless his holy name forever and ever.
\c 146
\s1 Praise Yahweh the liberator
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yah.
\q1 I’ll praise Yahweh with my whole being.
\q1
\v 2 I’ll praise Yahweh with my life.
\q1 I’ll sing praises to my god as long as I live.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Don’t trust in politicians
\q1 or in people—none of them can save you.
\q1
\v 4 When their breath ceases, they return to the ground—
\q1 \add all\add* their plans come to an end at that time.
\b
\q1
\v 5 Blessed are the people who have Jacob’s god to help them—
\q1 whose hope is in their God Yahweh:
\q1
\v 6 He’s the one who made the heavens and earth, the sea, and all that is in them.
\q1 He’s the one who remains faithful forever.
\q1
\v 7 He’s the one who executes justice for the oppressed.
\q1 He’s the one who gives food to the hungry.
\b
\q1 Yahweh liberates the prisoners.
\q1
\v 8 Yahweh opens the eyes of the blind.
\q1 Yahweh straightens up those who are bowed down \add under heavy loads\add*.
\q1 Yahweh loves those who do what is right.
\q1
\v 9 Yahweh guards the refugees.
\q1 He sustains orphans and widows,
\q1 but he subverts the plans of the wicked.
\q1
\v 10 Yahweh reigns forever.
\q1 Zion, your god reigns throughout the generations.
\b
\q1 Praise Yahweh.
\c 147
\s1 Praise Yahweh who’s in control
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh.
\b
\q1 How good it is to sing praises to our god,
\q1 How pleasant and suitable is a praise song.
\q1
\v 2 Yahweh builds up Jerusalem,
\q1 He gathers the Israelites who were taken to other countries.
\q1
\v 3 He heals those who are broken-hearted
\q1 and bandages their wounds.
\b
\q1
\v 4 He counts the stars—
\q1 he calls all of them by name.
\q1
\v 5 Our master is wonderful and very powerful.
\q1 His understanding is unlimited.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh helps the oppressed up.
\q1 He brings down the wicked to the ground.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Sing to Yahweh with thanksgiving.
\q1 Make music for our god with a harp.
\q1
\v 8 He’s the one who covers the skies with clouds.
\q1 He’s the one who prepares rain for the earth.
\q1 He’s the one who makes the mountains sprout with grass.
\q1
\v 9 He gives food to the animals
\q1 and to the ravens’ chicks when they squawk.
\b
\q1
\v 10 He doesn’t delight in the strength of a horse,
\q1 He doesn’t enjoy a man’s legs.
\q1
\v 11 Yahweh enjoys those who fear him—
\q1 those who know to expect his loyal commitment.
\b
\q1
\v 12 Tell about Yahweh’s greatness, Jerusalem.
\q1 Praise your god, Zion,
\q1
\v 13 because he strengthens the \add locking\add* bars on your gates.
\q1 He blesses your children there in the city.
\q1
\v 14 He give peace on your borders.
\q1 He satisfies you with excellent grain.
\q1
\v 15 He sends out his command to the soil,
\q1 His message travels quickly.
\q1
\v 16 He makes the snow \add coat the ground\add* like wool \add on a sheep\add*.
\q1 He scatters the frost \add across the countryside\add* like \add people scatter\add* ashes.
\q1
\v 17 He sends out his hail like crumbs \add brushed off a table\add*.
\q1 Who can stand before his cold?
\q1
\v 18 He sends out his command and melts them.
\q1 He makes his wind blow, and the waters flow.
\b
\q1
\v 19 He declared his message to Jacob—
\q1 his instructions and his regulations for Israel.
\q1
\v 20 He hasn’t done that for any other country,
\q1 and as for his instructions, they don’t know them.
\b
\q1 Praise Yahweh.
\c 148
\s1 Praise Yahweh everyone
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh.
\b
\q1 Praise Yahweh from the skies.
\q1 Praise him in the heights.
\q1
\v 2 Praise him, all his messengers.
\q1 Praise him, all his armies.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Praise him, sun and moon.
\q1 Praise him, all you shining stars.
\q1
\v 4 Praise him, highest heavens
\q1 and the waters that are above the heavens.
\b
\q1
\v 5 Let them praise Yahweh’s name,
\q1 because he gave the command and then they were created.
\q1
\v 6 He has established them forever and ever.
\q1 He made a decree and it won’t \add ever\add* become invalid.
\b
\q1
\v 7 Praise Yahweh from the earth,
\q1 you sea monsters and all ocean depths,
\q1
\v 8 fire and hail, snow and clouds,
\q1 stormy winds doing what he commanded.
\b
\q1
\v 9 The mountains and all the hills,
\q1 the fruit trees and all the cedars,
\q1
\v 10 \add both\add* wild and all the domestic animals,
\q1 reptiles and winged birds.
\b
\q1
\v 11 Kings across the world and all the peoples,
\q1 princes and all judges across the world,
\q1
\v 12 young men and also young women,
\q1 the elderly along with the children,
\q1
\v 13 let them all praise Yahweh’s name,
\q1 because his name alone is famous.
\q1 His splendour extends over the earth and \add in\add* the heavens.
\q1
\v 14 He has increased his people’s military strength.
\q1 Praise from all his faithful ones—
\q1 from Israel’s descendants—the people near to him.
\b
\q1 Praise Yahweh.
\c 149
\s1 A song of praise
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh.
\b
\q1 Sing a new song to Yahweh.
\q1 Praise him in the assembly of the faithful ones.
\q1
\v 2 Let Israel’s \add people\add* be glad about the one who made them.
\q1 Let Zion’s children be glad about their king.
\q1
\v 3 Let them praise him with dancing—
\q1 making music with tambourines and harps,
\q1
\v 4 because Yahweh appreciates his people,
\q1 and he honours humble people by saving them.
\q1
\v 5 That honour will make the faithful people cheerful,
\q1 and they’ll happily sing during the night.
\q1
\v 6 They should use their mouths to praise God,
\q1 but hold sharp swords in their hands
\q1
\v 7 to inflict vengeance on the nations
\q1 and punishment on the people groups,
\q1
\v 8 to tie their kings up with chains
\q1 and to put their leading citizens into iron shackles.
\q1
\v 9 \add They’ll\add* carry out the written judgements against them.
\q1 That’ll be an honour for all his faithful ones.
\b
\q1 Praise Yahweh.
\c 150
\s1 Praise Yahweh everyone
\q1
\v 1 Praise Yahweh.
\b
\q1 Praise God in his residence of holiness.
\q1 Praise him for his power in heaven.
\q1
\v 2 Praise him for his powerful actions.
\q1 Praise him because he’s so influential.
\b
\q1
\v 3 Praise him with a trumpet blast.
\q1 Praise him with the ukelele and harp.
\q1
\v 4 Praise him with tambourines and dancing.
\q1 Praise him with stringed instruments and flutes.
\q1
\v 5 Praise him with vibrating cymbals.
\q1 Praise him with clanging cymbals.
\b
\q1
\v 6 Let everything that can breathe praise Yahweh.
\b
\q1 Praise Yahweh.
